DEVICE  : 2026 Hacker Badge, ESP32-C3-MINI-1, USB-Serial-JTAG (VID_303A PID_1001) on COM8, through a dongle
CAPTURED: 2026-09-20T07:16:50-04:00

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THE BADGE ITSELF SAID
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. THE FLASH WORKS, WITHOUT DOWNLOAD MODE

    Wrote 743856 bytes (452426 compressed) at 0x00010000 in 2.5 seconds (effective 2337.5 kbit/s)...
    Hash of data verified.

   The ROM bootloader accepted and verified the image. No Start-button hold was needed for any of the
   four flashes in this session; the guide's download-mode step is for boards whose USB does not reset
   the chip, and this one does.

2. THE FIRST FIRMWARE RESET-LOOPED AT RADIO START

   Nineteen boots in thirty seconds, each one ending in the same place (captured verbatim, one boot):

    badge rc controller: 4 buttons -> ESP-NOW -> car
    reset reason: BROWNOUT (supply dipped) (9)
    [i2c] SC7A20 ok, -1 168 1028 mg (WHO_AM_I 0x11)
    [espnow] starting radio...

   The reset reason is the chip's own register, read on the next boot: BROWNOUT on every alternating
   cycle, POWERON (1) on the others - the rail collapsed hard enough that some resets looked like a
   power cycle. Adding that one line to setup() is what turned "it keeps restarting" into a fact.

3. WHAT DID SURVIVE, ON THE SAME DONGLE

   With the radio skipped at boot (BADGE_RADIO_ON_BOOT=0), the badge is stable and everything except
   the link works - this is one session, verbatim:

    [state] cmd=STOP inject=no buttons=AUX1
    [state] radio=DOWN car=none age=0ms rx=0 tx=0 fails=0 carcmd=0,0 flags=0x00 seq=0 uptime=0s
    [inject] FWD
    [cmd] FWD (buttons AUX1)
    [state] cmd=FWD inject=FWD buttons=AUX1
    [inject] STOP
    [cmd] STOP (buttons AUX1)
    [inject] cleared

   So on real hardware: the 74HC165 read works (and AUX1 reads pressed, which is the guide's "Aux1 is
   a maintained side switch, not momentary - don't assume it boots released"), the debounce and the
   console both work, the maneuver state machine works, and the panel/LED draw paths run without
   faulting. And the radio can be started on demand:

    [espnow] starting radio...
    [espnow] max tx power set to 8 (2.0 dBm)
    [espnow] channel 1, pair key 0x5A17BAD6, badge mac 28:84:85:EA:C9:BC
    [state] cmd=STOP inject=no buttons=AUX1
    [state] radio=up car=none age=0ms rx=0 tx=0 fails=0 carcmd=0,0 flags=0x00 seq=0 uptime=0s

   The radio initialises, reports up, and the badge survives it. What it does NOT survive is
   transmitting: injecting a direction (the 20 Hz stream) dropped the device within a few seconds, and
   so did the retry at 5 Hz with the TX power already at the API floor:

    [inject] FWD
    [cmd] FWD (buttons AUX1)
    <device gone: ClearCommError, then the port disappears and it boots again>

4. WHY SOFTWARE CANNOT FIX THIS ONE

   Transmit power is already at the bottom of the range the API accepts (2 dBm - 8 in 0.25 dBm units),
   the packet rate is a fifth of the car's deadman requirement, the CPU runs at 80 MHz and the panel
   refreshes at 4 Hz. The remaining current is the receiver, and the SDK is explicit about it:

     esp_now.h:  "If never configured wake_window, the chip would keep waked at disconnected once it
                  uses esp_now"                                        (attention 5 to set_wake_window)
     esp_now.h:  "Only when ESP_WIFI_STA_DISCONNECTED_PM_ENABLE is enabled, this configuration could
                  work"                                                (attention 1)
     sdkconfig (the ESP32-C3 libraries this core ships):
                 "# CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_STA_DISCONNECTED_PM_ENABLE is not set"

   So the wake window cannot be used in this build, and a disconnected ESP-NOW station stays awake:
   roughly 100 mA of receiver on top of the panel, which is what this dongle's rail cannot carry. That
   is a supply limit, not a software one. The two ways out are power (a port that can source ~500 mA
   peaks, or charged AAs in the holder) or a lighter radio; the car already speaks BLE for the phone
   app, which is the obvious second candidate, at the cost of a Bluedroid stack this board's heap may
   not like (guide section 8).

